<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · song</p><h1 class="enc-title">We'll Meet Again</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1939">1939</a>; first recorded 28 September <a href="/years/1939">1939</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">&quot;We'll Meet Again&quot; was written in 1939 by the English songwriters Ross Parker and Hughie Charles and published by Michael Ross Limited, whose directors included Parker; the pianist Norman Keen collaborated with them on it.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The song was first recorded by Vera Lynn with Arthur Young, who accompanied her on the Hammond Novachord, an early electronic keyboard. It was written in D major at a tempo of 92 beats a minute. It became one of the best-known songs of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">World War II</a>, speaking to servicemen leaving to fight and to the families they left behind, and it gave its name to the 1943 musical film We'll Meet Again, in which Lynn starred in a loose version of her own life as a forces' sweetheart.</p><p>Lynn recorded it again in 1953 with fuller instrumentation and a chorus of British Armed Forces personnel, and that version reached number 29 on the American charts in 1954. The song has been credited as one of the first to use the Novachord, the first polyphonic synthesizer.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>During the Cold War Lynn's recording was among the music kept in 20 underground radio stations of the BBC's Wartime Broadcasting Service, meant to provide information and raise morale for 100 days after a nuclear attack. Stanley Kubrick used her 1953 recording, with bitter irony, over the nuclear destruction that ends his 1964 film Dr. Strangelove. The Byrds closed their debut album Mr. Tambourine Man with it, and Pink Floyd's song Vera on The Wall of 1979 refers to its words and to Lynn.</p><p>It is traditionally played on 5 May to close the Liberation Day Concert in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amsterdam">Amsterdam</a>, marking the end of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">World War II</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a>. Lynn sang it in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> in 2005 on the 60th anniversary of VE Day. On 5 April 2020 Queen <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elizabeth-ii">Elizabeth II</a> referred to it in a televised address during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/covid-19-pandemic">COVID-19 pandemic</a>; that year a duet of Lynn and Katherine Jenkins, first released in 2014, reached number 72 on the UK Singles Chart for NHS charities, and after the 75th anniversary of VE Day Lynn's solo version reached number 55.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “We'll Meet Again” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
